## a b s t r a c t
Recently, Ni, N-doped carbon (NiANAC) electrocatalysts synthesized using metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as templates have demonstrated attractive catalytic performances in the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R})$ . However, most of the reported preparations of MOFs-based precursors are carried out in organic solvents, and the resulting ${\mathsf{N i}}{\mathrm{-N-C}}$ materials have relatively low metal loadings and mainly exhibit microporous structures, which is unfavorable for the mass transport. Herein, Ni, N-doped mesomicroporous carbon electrocatalysts with a range of Ni loadings $(\mathsf{M}-\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N-}\mathsf{C-}\mathsf{x}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{T}s)$ were prepared by the pyrolysis of MOFs-based precursors synthesized in aqueous solution using the surfactant cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) as a modifier to promote the adsorption of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ions and the formation of mesopores. Owing to the unique morphology, porous structure and high contents of $\mathsf{N i-N_{x}}$ sites and pyrrolic-N, the optimal catalyst $(\mathsf{M}-\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N-}\mathsf{C-}2/\mathsf{C N T s})$ shows superior electrocatalytic activity for the $C O_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ with a maximum CO Faradaic efficiency $(\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}})$ of $98\ \%$ at $-0.7~\mathrm{V}$ vs. reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), and the $\mathtt{F E}_{\mathtt{C O}}$ can reach over $80\%$ in a wide potential range of $-0.5$ to $-1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE. This work develops a facile and environmentally friendly strategy to obtain highperformance and low-cost transition metal-nitrogen-doped porous carbon electrocatalysts for the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\mathsf{R R}}.$ $\circledcirc$ 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
## 2. Results and discussion
Transition metal-nitrogen-doped carbon $(\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{C})$ catalysts have emerged as promising materials for the electrocatalytic carbon dioxide reduction reaction $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R})$ [1-5]. The preparation strategies of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}-\mathbf{N}-C$ catalysts generally include wet-chemistry method, atomic layer deposition, pyrolysis of metal-N macrocycle compounds, and pyrolysis of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) precursors or polymers [6–10]. Among them, the strategy of pyrolysis of MOFs precursors has aroused much attention due to the distinct advantages of MOFs-derived MANAC materials: 1) The morphology and composition can be finely adjusted through changing the synthesis parameters; 2) the single metal sites and heteroatoms can be in-situ doped into the carbon skeleton by high-temperature pyrolysis of MOFs precursors; 3) the obtained carbon materials possess large specific surface areas, which can provide sufficient active sites [11]. In addition, in order to alleviate the agglomeration of metal atomic sites during direct pyrolysis of the MOFs-based precursors at high temperature, zinc-based zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) with flexible cavities has been used as a template for doping other metal species into the carbon frameworks. Due to the low boiling point of zinc metal $(907~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , the Zn species evaporate at high temperature, resulting in the generation of abundant N-rich defects. Then, other non-volatile metal species will be stabilized by coordinating with N, thus reducing the accumulation of metal species [4,12].
Among the reported MANAC materials, the NiANAC catalysts exhibit excellent selectivity for the electrochemical reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO [13-16]. The synthetic methods of MOFs-derived ${\mathsf{N i}}{\mathsf{-N-C}}$ mainly include post-treatment impregnation and in-situ adsorption methods [17,18]. The post-treatment impregnation approach usually suffers from tedious preparation process, and the metal loadings of the obtained carbon materials are relatively low, while the in-situ adsorption method is more convenient, because the metal salts are directly added to the precursor solutions of ZIF-8 to form the ZIF-8/metal composites. Unfortunately, most of the published work uses organic solvents (methanol or N, N-dimethylformamide) in the preparation process, thereby producing a large amount of toxic waste. Moreover, the surfaces of the carbon materials synthesized by the above methods mainly exhibits only abundant micropores. Although the micropores can offer a large surface area, they are not conducive to mass transfer, thus hindering the electrocatalytic performance. Hence, it is of great significance to explore a facile and environmentally friendly strategy to construct efficient Ni-N-doped carbon catalysts with hierarchically porous structure.
Herein, we develop a facile and green strategy that avoids the use of organic solvents to fabricate novel ZIF-8-derived Ni-N porous carbon catalysts with a range of Ni loadings (donated as $\mathsf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N i}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{x}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{s})$ for highly efficient electroreduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO. Specifically, the surfactant cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) was used as a modifier, which not only modified the hydrophobic surface of ZIF-8 to in-situ adsorb more $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ions through the coordinate effect, but also decomposed to form abundant mesopores during the high temperature pyrolysis process. The preparation process was carried out in aqueous solution, and the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ions were in-situ adsorbed on the surface of ZIF-8 through the coordination with CTAB. The electrochemical results demonstrate that the abundant $\mathsf{N i-N_{x}}$ single atomic sites and plentiful pyrrolic-N, as well as unique meso-microporous structure, jointly contribute to the high $C O_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ performance of the optimal catalyst $(\mathsf{M}-\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N-}{\mathsf{C-}}2/\mathsf{C N T s})$ , which exhibits a large CO partial current density of $-19.8\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ vs. reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) and a maximum CO Faradaic efficiency $(\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}})$ of 98 $\%$ at $-0.7\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE in the $\mathsf{H}$ -type cell.
The preparation procedure of MNiANACAx/CNTs and NiANAC/CNTs is shown in Fig. 1a. First, 2-methylimidazole and zinc nitrate hexahydrate were dissolved in an aqueous solution containing CTAB. CTAB would adsorb on the surface of ZIF-8 crystals and slow down the crystal growth rate, resulting in the formation of cubic morphological and small-sized ZIF-8. Different amounts of ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ were then added to the above solution as nickel source. Subsequently, the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ions coordinate with CTAB chains to form the ZIF $-8@C\mathrm{TAB@Ni}$ crystals during hydrothermal treatment. Finally, the ZI $\mathsf{F}{-}8@\mathsf{C T A B@N i}$ was pyrolyzed at $950~^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ followed by acid treatment to obtain the Ni-N doped porous carbon and carbon nanotubes, named $\mathsf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N i}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{x}/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{T}s$ (M refers to the CTAB modification, x is 1, 2, 3 to differentiate the catalysts synthesized by adding 0.1, 0.15 and $0.2\:\mathrm{mmol}$ of nickel source, respectively). The generation of carbon nanotubes may be due to the existence of Ni clusters formed by the migration of single Ni atoms at high temperature, which can act as catalytic centers to promote the growth of carbon nanotubes [17,19]. Without the addition of CTAB, the ZIF-8 with larger particle size and irregular morphology was obtained, and the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ions were adsorbed on the surface of ZIF-8 through hydrogen bonding and $\pi{-}\pi$ bonding interactions [20]. After the pyrolysis process and acid treatment, the Ni-N doped carbon particle and carbon nanotubes (denoted as NiANAC/CNTs) were obtained.
The morphology and structure of the as-prepared materials were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The ZIF-8@CTAB@Ni-2 has a uniform size of $\sim200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , inheriting the regular cube morphology of the original ZIF-8 (Figure S1a, c). After carbonization and acid treatment, the resulting M NiANACA2/CNTs catalyst still maintained the initial cube shape with hollow bamboo-like carbon nanotubes distributed around the cube (Fig. 1b). Furthermore, no nanoparticles can be seen in Fig. 1b. As shown in the higher-magnification TEM image (Fig. 1c), abundant mesopores formed by the pyrolysis of surfactant CTAB exist on the surface of the M NiANACA2/CNTs. In addition, the energy-dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping images reveal that C, N and Ni elements are homogeneously dispersed throughout the whole structure (Fig. 1d-g).
The ZIF-8@CTAB@Ni-1 and ZIF-8@CTAB@Ni-3 samples consist of regular cubes of about $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in size, and their morphologies are similar to that of ZIF- $-8@\mathrm{CTAB@Ni-2}$ (Figure S1b, d). After carbonization and acid treatment, the obtained M NiANACA1/CNTs and M NiANACA3/CNTs samples also exhibit cubic shapes accompanied by carbon nanotubes and have mesoporous structures on their surface. (Figure S2). However, as the amount of nickel increases, the amount of the carbon nanotubes increases, and the diameter of the carbon nanotubes widens. This may be due to that the Ni species with higher content tend to agglomerate into larger Ni nanoclusters during the carbonization process, thus providing catalytic centers for the generation of more and larger carbon nanotubes. The ZIF-8@Ni sample obtained without CTAB modification contains irregular particles of about $1{-}3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in size (Figure S3). The final carbonized sample $(\mathrm{Ni-N-C-2}/\mathrm{CNTs}),$ shows irregular shape accompanied by a large number of carbon nanotubes (Figure S4a). Moreover, no clear pore structure can be found on the surface of the NiANACA2/CNTs sample (Figure S4b).
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of ZIF-8, ZIF-8@CTAB@Ni and M NiANACA2/CNTs are shown in Fig. 2a. The XRD pattern of the as-prepared ZIF-8@CTAB@Ni matches well that of ZIF-8, indicating that the crystal structure of ZIF-8 does not change after $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ adsorption. In the XRD pattern of MNiANACA2/CNTs sample, only two broad peaks at $\sim26^{\circ}$ and $\sim43^{\circ}$ were observed, corresponding to the (002) and (101) planes of graphitic carbon, respectively [21,22]. In addition, the diffraction peaks in the XRD patterns of M NiANACA1/CNTs, M NiANACA3/CNTs and NiANACA2/CNTs are similar to those of MNiANACA2/CNTs (Figure S5). This suggests that there are no obvious metal particles in M NiANACAx/CNTs materials, which is in agreement with the TEM observations. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and porous structure of the samples were analyzed by nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms and pore size distribution curves. As depicted in Fig. 2b and Figure S6a, the nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms of $\sf M{-}\sf N i{-}\sf N{-}\sf C{-}\sf X/C\sf N T$ s show typical type IV isotherm characteristics with type H4 hysteresis loops, which reflects the existence of mesoporous structures [23]. The corresponding density-functional theory (DFT) pore size distribution curves demonstrate that the $\sf M{-}\sf N i{-}\sf N{-}\sf C{-}\sf x/C\sf N$ Ts samples have abundant mesopores in the range of $1{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S6b). However, the pore size distribution of NiANACA2/CNTs is mainly concentrated in the microporous region $(<1\ \mathrm{\nm})$ (Figure S6b), which is not conducive to mass transfer during the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\mathsf{R R}}.$ Furthermore, the BET surface area of MNiANACA2/CNTs $\mathbf{\bar{\Psi}}1026.1\mathbf{m}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ) is larger than that of MNiANACA1/CNTs $(750.4~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , MNiANACA3/CNTs $(808.8\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~\bf~g}^{-1})$ and NiANACA2/CNTs $(907.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{\bf~g}^{-1}.$ .
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was further carried out to characterize the surface chemical compositions of materials. The XPS survey spectra reveal the existence of C, N, O and Ni elements in the MNiANACAx/CNTs and NiANACA2/CNTs samples (Figure S7). The relative percentages of these elements are listed in Table S1. As shown in Fig. 2c and Figure S8a-c, the highresolution N 1 s spectra of M NiANACAx/CNTs and $_{\mathsf{N i-N-C-}2|}$ CNTs can be fitted into five peaks appearing at 398.4, 399.5, 400.5, 401.7 and $403.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which can be attributed to pyridinic N, Ni-N, pyrrolic N, graphitic N and oxidic N, respectively [24- 26]. The percentages of different N species for all the catalysts are displayed in Table S2. In addition, two main peaks at $855.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $873.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ are observed in the high-resolution $\mathsf{N i2p}$ spectra, corresponding to the Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively (Fig. 2d and Figure S8d-f). The appearance of the peak of Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ at 855.0 eV indicates that the valence state of Ni in the M NiANACAx/CNTs and NiANACA2/CNTs catalysts is between metallic ${\mathsf{N i}}^{0}$ $853.5~\mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ (856.0 eV) [27]. Notably, among these investigated catalysts, the $\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{C}{-}2/\mathsf{C N I}$ s shows the highest Ni content $(0.74\mathrm{at\%})$ . It is worth noting that the Ni content of M NiANACA3/CNTs is lower than that of $\mathsf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N i}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}2/\mathsf{C}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{s},$ · which may be due to that more nickel atoms aggregate into clusters or particles during carbonization when the amount of Ni precursor increases, and then these as-formed clusters or particles are finally removed by acid treatment, resulting in a decrease of nickel content in the catalyst. In addition, the Ni content of NiANACA2/CNTs (0.55 at%) is much lower than that of MNiANACA2/CNTs, indicating that the CTAB has a positive effect on $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ion adsorption.
The Raman spectra (Figure S9) of $\mathsf{M}{\mathrm{-Ni}}{\mathrm{-N-C-x/CNTs}}$ and NiANACA2/CNTs show two carbon signal peaks at $1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigned to the D band and $1580\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigned to the G band, corresponding to the disordered/defective carbon and graphitic carbon, respectively. The intensity ratio of the D band to G band $\mathrm{(I_{D}/}$ $|\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\rho}}_{\mathrm{I_{G}}}\rangle$ of M NiANACA2/CNTs was calculated to be 1.17, which is higher than that of $\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{C}{-}1/$ CNTs (1.05), $\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{C}{-}3/$ CNTs (1.09) and NiANACA2/CNTs (1.08), indicating its rich defective structure [28,29].
The electrochemical ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction performances of the M NiANACAx/CNTs and NiANACA2/CNTs catalysts were investigated in an H-type cell with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ as the electrolyte. Fig. 3a shows the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of the $\mathsf{M}{\mathrm{-Ni}}{\mathrm{-N-C-x}}/\mathsf{C N T s}$ and NiANACA2/CNTs catalysts in the potential range of 0 to $-1.0\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE under ${\sf N}_{2}$ or ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ saturated 0.1 M ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ aqueous solution. The M NiANACA2/CNTs exhibits a large current density of $-25.1\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at $-1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ atmosphere, which is higher than that of $\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{C}{-}1/\mathsf{C N}^{-}$ Ts $(-16.9\mathrm{\quad}\mathrm{mA\quad}\mathrm{cm^{-2}};$ , $\mathsf{M}\mathrm{-Ni}{\mathrm{-N-C-}}3/\mathrm{CNTs}$ $(-21.8\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) and NiANACA2/CNTs $(-20.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ). The current density of $\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{C}{-}2/\mathsf{C N T s}$ in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ atmosphere is extremely larger than that in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere $(-5.1\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{\cm}^{-2})$ , confirming its intrinsic activity towards ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}.$ . The gaseous and liquid products were analyzed by gas chromatograph (GC) and $\mathrm{^1H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, respectively. It can be found that only the gaseous products of CO and $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ were detected, and no liquid products were detected (Figure S10). The Faradaic efficiencies of CO $\mathrm{\DeltaFE_{CO})}$ of all the catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\mathsf{R R}}$ were evaluated in the potential range of $-0.5\mathrm{~V~}\sim-1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig. 3b). At $-0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE, all the catalysts reach the highest $\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}}$ , and M NiANACA2/CNTs achieves a maximum $\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}}$ of $98\ \%$ . Particularly, the $\mathtt{F E}_{\mathtt{C O}}$ of M NiANACA2/CNTs exceeds $80\%$ within a wide potential window of $-0.5\sim-1.0\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE, while the $\mathtt{F E}_{\mathtt{C O}}$ of other catalysts shows a clear volcanic trend, suggesting that the M NiANACA2/CNTs catalyst possesses a desirable selectivity for CO. As shown in Fig. 3c, we can calculate the CO partial current density $\left(\mathrm{J}_{\mathsf{C O}}\right)$ of different catalysts based on the FE of CO. The $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Jco}$ of $\mathrm{M-Ni-N-C-2/CNTs}$ s is as high as $-19.8\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at $-1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE, which is 2.6, 1.3 and 3.6 times that of $\mathsf{M}{\mathrm{-Ni}{\mathrm{-N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}1}/$ CNTs, M NiANACA3/CNTs and $\mathsf{N i-N-C-2}/\mathsf{C N T s}$ , respectively.
water is used as the solvent to avoid the use of organic solvents, and the surfactant CTAB is used as a modifier to facilitate the adsorption of Ni ions and the formation of mesopores. For the electrocatalytic $C0_{2}\tt R R$ , the optimum catalyst $(\mathrm{M}{-}\mathrm{Ni}{-}\mathrm{N-}\mathrm{C-}2/\mathrm{CNTs})$ achieves a maximum $\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}}$ of $98\%$ at $-0.7\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE, a large CO partial current density of $-19.8\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and a wide potential window of $500\mathrm{mV}$ for $\mathtt{F E}_{\mathtt{C O}}$ over $80\%$ , as well as excellent stability, outperforming the M NiANACA1/CNTs, M NiANACA3/CNTs and NiANACA2/CNTs catalysts. The excellent $C O_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ performance is attributed to the unique meso-microprous structure, rich defects, abundant $\mathsf{N i-N_{x}}$ sites, and high content of pyrrolic-N, which synergistically contribute to the enhancement of the activity and selectivity of the $\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{C}{-}2/\mathsf{C N}$ Ts catalyst. This work not only offers an advanced Ni-N doped porous carbon catalyst for the electroreduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO, but also, more importantly, provides a promising environmental and facile approach for designing and constructing highly efficient and low-cost transition metalnitrogen-doped porous carbon catalysts.
